From 2882bcf92c6bfbe3993f8552098927dab6d6f960 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work5 <>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 05:39:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drivers/crashrpt: capture application diagnostics before
 assert handling

Adds a mechanism that lets an application publish diagnostic information at
the moment the system is about to assert, so it can be written to a file
before the board reboots.

Design
------

The application registers a high priority reporter thread through
/dev/crashrpt and parks it in fin_wait().  At the very first statement of
up_assert(), before any crash handling begins, the kernel wakes that thread
and blocks on a completion semaphore until it signals that it has finished.
Assert handling then proceeds exactly as before.

Hook placement is what makes this work.  Everything further down in
up_assert() - the critical section, up_cpu_pause_all(), the abort state -
makes it impossible to run an application thread.  At the top of the
function, for an assert raised by the application itself, the system is
still healthy: interrupts are enabled, the scheduler is running and the
application memory context is loaded.  An application assert reaches
up_assert() through the ordinary system call dispatch path, so nothing has
broken by the time the hook runs.

The reason code passed to the reporter reuses the three way classification
already present in arm_assert.c, reading the two location globals without
clearing them so the existing logic is unaffected.

Points that matter in the implementation
----------------------------------------

The re-entry guard is taken before any other work.  up_assert() has not yet
recorded its abort state when the hook runs, so a fault raised by the
reporter would be seen as a fresh assert and would re-enter indefinitely.

fin_notify() is used to wake the reporter because it calls the ordinary
up_unblock_task(), which saves the asserting context so it resumes once the
reporter is done.  up_unblock_task_without_savereg() must not be used here -
that variant exists to discard a broken context on the real crash path and
would destroy the thread that has to continue and reboot the board.

sem_tickwait() is used rather than sem_timedwait() so the wait does not
depend on the clock_gettime() path.

The reporter is boosted to maximum priority before the wake.  This is a
correctness requirement rather than a latency optimisation - without it the
scheduler may not select the reporter and the deadline is consumed doing
nothing.

crashrpt_arm_watchdog() is a weak no-op that boards should override.  The
software deadline relies on a working scheduler, which cannot be assumed
once something has already gone wrong.

Not served by this path
-----------------------

Hardware faults and asserts raised from interrupt context are detected and
skipped, because the abort handlers have already taken the exception context
and the scheduler cannot run the reporter.  Those cases need data that was
published before the fault.

Disabled by default.  Enable with CONFIG_CRASH_REPORT.
---
 os/arch/arm/src/armv7-a/arm_assert.c     |  28 ++
 os/arch/arm/src/armv7-a/arm_initialize.c |   8 +
 os/drivers/Kconfig                       |  35 +++
 os/drivers/Makefile                      |   4 +
 os/drivers/crashrpt.c                    | 341 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 os/include/tinyara/crashrpt.h            | 202 ++++++++++++++
 os/include/tinyara/fs/ioctl.h            |   6 +
 7 files changed, 624 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 os/drivers/crashrpt.c
 create mode 100644 os/include/tinyara/crashrpt.h

diff --git a/os/arch/arm/src/armv7-a/arm_assert.c b/os/arch/arm/src/armv7-a/arm_assert.c
index 5d01ee26..97e996f2 100644
--- a/os/arch/arm/src/armv7-a/arm_assert.c
+++ b/os/arch/arm/src/armv7-a/arm_assert.c
@@ -82,6 +82,9 @@
 #include <tinyara/binfmt/elf.h>
 #endif
 #include <tinyara/security_level.h>
+#ifdef CONFIG_CRASH_REPORT
+#include <tinyara/crashrpt.h>
+#endif
 #ifdef CONFIG_SYSTEM_REBOOT_REASON
 #include <arch/reboot_reason.h>
 #endif
@@ -582,6 +585,31 @@ static inline void print_assert_detail(const uint8_t *filename, int lineno, stru
 
 void up_assert(const uint8_t *filename, int lineno)
 {
+#ifdef CONFIG_CRASH_REPORT
+	/* Capture application diagnostics before any assert handling begins.
+	 *
+	 * This has to be the very first statement in the function.  Everything
+	 * below - the critical section, up_cpu_pause_all(), the abort state -
+	 * makes it impossible to run an application thread.  Here, for an assert
+	 * raised by the application itself, interrupts are still enabled, the
+	 * scheduler is running and the application's memory context is loaded.
+	 *
+	 * crashrpt_notify() blocks until the reporter is done or the deadline
+	 * expires, then returns and we continue exactly as before.  It takes its
+	 * own re-entry guard, so a fault inside the reporter cannot loop back
+	 * into here.
+	 */
+
+	int crash_reason = CRASHRPT_REASON_KERNEL_ASSERT;
+
+	if (system_exception_location) {
+		crash_reason = CRASHRPT_REASON_HW_FAULT;
+	} else if (user_assert_location) {
+		crash_reason = CRASHRPT_REASON_APP_ASSERT;
+	}
+
+	(void)crashrpt_notify(crash_reason);
+#endif
 
 	irqstate_t flags = enter_critical_section();
 
